{"author":{"id":"417295a7cc0952a9a38b67be3a894daed91f9069f7407e038d12a4f9b9a8451e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792347395,"nonce":"gvyKsJvuMKqYeu4FRtXzBGYS2hM=","title":"RayScript host process: one extension per process, JSON lines on stdio","message":"What exists (crates/rayscript-host): the `rayscript-host [--root DIR]` binary, `Host` (testable without a process) and `serve`. The protocol is written out in the crate docs: messages from the editor (module, run, event, resolve, reject, describe, shutdown) and from the host (ready, ops, call, log, error, describe).\n\nDecisions\n- One extension, one process, one VM. A runaway script or a native fault ends only that extension, the editor can kill it, and there is no cross-VM value passing. A VM is not Send, so this also avoids sharing one.\n- JSON lines: easy to read, to log and to replay, and `Json.parse` exists on the Haxe side. Operations are arrays (`[\"set\",7,\"class\",\"a\"]`) to keep a 10,000-row mount small. Elements are numbered by the host and ids are not reused. Groups take ids too, so ids have gaps.\n- A remote module is a list of function names the editor registers before `run`. Each function, called, sends a `call` and returns a promise; the script `await`s it. The reply is `resolve` or `reject`. Operations made before a call are sent before it. A host function used as an event handler is called directly.\n- The reader thread moves lines into a channel, so the host never blocks on input and the editor cannot deadlock by writing while the host writes. The editor must read standard output.\n- A turn of the loop: take every line waiting, give fibers and effects a 4 ms slice, send what was made. When nothing is ready it sleeps until the next line or the next timer. A script that spins does not stop the host listening (tested).\n- `trace` goes to a `log` message; standard output carries only protocol. Errors carry text for a person and `items` with line, column and function.\n- `sleep(ms)` is a promise resolved by the host loop.\n- The thread running the VM has a 64 MiB stack.\n\nKnown gaps\n- A fiber started with `spawn` and never awaited loses its failure; only the main script and event handlers are reported.\n- No fuel budget for calls made from effects (06707e2): an endless effect blocks the loop, unlike an endless main script.\n- Remote modules are functions only, no constants or objects. No cancel message for a pending call; no way to ask the editor for the call's result with a timeout.\n- Module files are read from `--root` with names limited to letters, digits, `_` and `-`. No capability checks yet: the manifest and the editor decide what to register.\n\nNext: the Haxe client in RayDE (spawn, read lines without blocking the UI, apply ops to ashui through a tag registry, send events), tracked in RayDE's 971d58e.","files":null}]}